    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Haiku4.5 cast Knight of the White Orchid before playing their Plains land drop. Knight of the White Orchid's ETB ability checks if an opponent controls more lands than you. With 6 lands in play (and a Plains in hand unplayed), they have fewer lands than some opponents, so the trigger might still work. However, the correct sequencing consideration is that they should play the Plains first only if they already have equal or more lands than all opponents (to not miss the trigger). Looking at the board: Haiku4.5 has 6 lands, Gem2.5Flash has 5 lands, Sonnet4.5 has 5 lands, o4-mini has 6 lands. Since opponents have at most 6 lands (tied with Haiku4.5), playing Plains first would give Haiku4.5 7 lands, making them have MORE lands than all opponents, which would cause Knight's ETB to fail. So casting Knight first (at 6 lands, tied with o4-mini who has 6) means Knight checks and sees o4-mini has 6 lands = Haiku4.5's 6 lands, so the condition fails (opponent needs MORE lands). Actually, the issue is more about casting the creature before combat - Haiku4.5 has Garna giving other creatures haste, and Xathrid Necromancer on board. But Knight of the White Orchid is a 2/2 that could attack with haste from Garna. However, casting creatures before combat telegraphs your board state. More importantly, they should play Plains land AFTER casting Knight to not affect the ETB check. Actually with 6 lands and opponents having 5-6, the ETB won't trigger regardless since no opponent has strictly more. So the land play order doesn't matter for Knight. But they should consider playing Outpost Siege (a card advantage engine) instead, or at minimum play the land first then consider options. The play is reasonable given Garna's haste enabling and Path of Ancestry scry trigger.",
      "action_taken": "Cast Knight of the White Orchid before playing Plains land and before combat",
      "better_line": "Play Plains first (it doesn't affect Knight's ETB since no opponent has strictly more lands anyway), then consider whether Outpost Siege (a repeatable card advantage engine) might be more impactful than a 2/2 body. Alternatively, if wanting to attack with Knight via Garna's haste, casting before combat is fine, but should still play land first.",
